# Factory-tree /etc/shadow. root ships LOCKED here on purpose: the actual
# bootstrap password is a system credential, so it can be replaced per host
# from the ESP before the machine has ever booted.
#
#   /usr/lib/credstore/passwd.hashed-password.root   image default (changeme)
#   $ESP/loader/credentials/passwd.hashed-password.root.cred   per-host override
#
# systemd-firstboot treats a locked root ("!" in the second field) as "password
# not set" and applies the credential on first boot, writing the result to the
# /etc overlay upper. It is gated on ConditionFirstBoot=yes, so it can never
# clobber a later `passwd root` — that edit copies the file up and persists
# across image upgrades, which is exactly what we want.
#
# Shipping a real hash HERE instead would make the credential unreachable:
# firstboot would see a password already set and skip it forever.
#
# NINE colon-separated fields. The third (lastchg) is deliberately EMPTY, which
# disables password aging and keeps the build reproducible — any real date would
# have to be recomputed per build. An 8-field line shifts min/max/warn left:
# lastchg=0 means "must change at next login", min=99999 then refuses that
# change, and login becomes impossible. That shipped for one release.
#
# Console-only either way: sshd is publickey-only, and the documented flow ends
# in `passwd -l root`. Interactive users are declarative — see
# credentials/README.md for sysusers.extra and home.create.<user>.
root:!*::0:99999:7:::
